## 1. INTRODUCTION
Hydrogen, as a clean renewable energy source of fuel, is one of the most promising energy carriers for replacing traditional fossil fuels. Water electrolysis driven by renewable resource-derived electricity and water splitting driven by solar-energy photocatalysis are promising pathways for sustainable hydrogen production.1 Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) requires high-performance electrocatalyst/cocatalyst to promote the sluggish kinetics. Pt is the most active HER catalyst, but the concerns related to its higher cost and rarity are big hurdles for practical application.1c,d,f Therefore, numerous efforts have been devoted to exploring stable and noble-metal-free HER catalyst with good efficiency. In this regard, transition metal sulfides have been recently suggested as promising catalyst for HER,2 inspired by the structure/composition of nitrogenase and hydrogenase.3 But their activity is far from satisfied compared with noble Pt catalyst, and most of them work only in acidic media. Enhancing the electrical conductivity and increasing the number of exposed surface active sites are effective ways to improve the activity.4 Especially, homo-bimetallic sulfides, different from surface doping and heterojunction at surface/interface, are believed to manipulate the inherent electronic and/or surface structures of the host material toward better activity more effectively.2c,5 Moreover, very recent work shows that doping metal sulfides with secondary metal ions may produce catalyst that can work in alkaline and neutral medium.6 However, such homogenous composition is hard to achieve mainly due to the difficulty in preparing homo-bimetallic precursors.
Porous/hollow structures have attracted many attentions in catalysis due to the intriguing features such as large surface area, low density, kinetically favorable open structure and surface permeability. But abrication of hollow nanomaterials with delicately controlled composition and morphology is still a challenge. Hollow micro-/nanostructures can be produced using template method including hard, sacrificial and soft templates.8 Specifically, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising sacrificial template or self-template to construct hollow or porous structures.9 Moreover, the good compatibility of organic frameworks with various metal ions makes it easy to synthesize homogenous multi-metallic MOFs. With them as precursor, it is possible to fabricate homogenous multi-metallic materials with controllable composition.
Here we report a generalized method to synthesize hollow bimetallic sulfides $(\mathrm{M}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{S}_{4})$ with homogenous composition (Scheme 1). Homogenous bimetallic MOFs are used as self-templates and precursors to synthesize bimetallic sulfides via solvothermal sulfidation reaction and thermal annealing. Importantly, we also report $\mathrm{M}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , especially $\mathrm{{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{{S}_{4}}}}$ , shows excellent electrochemical HER activity better than most of the reported non-noble metal catalysts over a wide $\mathsf{p H}$ range $\mathrm{(pH=0-14)}$ ). Moreover, the material also demonstrates excellent photocatalytic HER activity that is comparable to Pt. Note that pristine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ is not a good HER catalyst compared with other sulfides. However, the activity is improved to a superior level due to the synergetic effect between homogenous incorporation of second metals in the lattice and formation of hollow structure.
## 2. EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
2.1. Materials. Eosin Y (EY), $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}_{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ , $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and thioacetamide (TAA) were obtained from J&K Chemical. Methanol, absolute ethanol and isopropanol were purchased from Tianjin Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute. Nafion solution $(5\mathrm{\wt\%})$ and 2-methylimidazole were obtained from Sigma Aldrich. All the chemicals are reagent grade and used as received. High purity water $18.25~\mathrm{M}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm})$ supplied by a UP Water Purification System was used in all the experiments.
2.2. Synthesis of Hollow $\mathbf{M}_{x}\mathbf{Co}_{3-x}\mathbf{S}_{4}$ Polyhedra. The synthesis of MCo-MOFs is similar to literatures that can be seen in supporting information (SI).9d,e Then $80~\mathrm{\mg}$ of MCo-MOFs was re-dispersed into $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol, followed by the addition of $0.12~\mathrm{g}$ of TAA. Thereafter, the mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~4~h~}$ . The formed $\mathrm{M}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ hollow polyhedra were centrifuged and washed with ethanol for several times. Finally they were annealed under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ .
2.3. Characterization. X-ray diffraction (XRD) was recorded with a Bruker D8 Focus operating at $40~\mathrm{kV}$ and 40 mA equipped with a nickel-filtered $\mathrm{{Cu}\ K a}$ radiation $(\lambda\:=$ 1.54056 Å). Field emission-scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) images were obtained using a Hitachi S-4800 SEM. TEM images and corresponding electron energy loss spectroscopy elemental mapping data (EELS) were obtained using a JEM-2100F transmission electron microscope at 200 $\mathbf{kV}$ . Element composition was analyzed using a VISTA-MPX EL02115765 inductive coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometer (ICP AES). BET surface area was determined using ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm measurements at - $-196~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ on a Micrometrics TriStar 3000 equipment. UV-vis diffuse reflectance spectra (UV-vis DRS) were recorded with a Hitachi U-3010 spectrometer using $\mathrm{BaSO_{4}}$ as the reference. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) data were obtained
