on a PHI-1600 instrument equipped with Al Kα radiation.
2.4. Electrocatalytic HER. All the electrochemical measurements were conducted using an IVIUMSTAT working station (Ivium Technologies BV, Netherlands) in a typical three-electrode setup with an electrolyte solution, a graphite rod as the counter electrode and a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) as the reference electrode. The reference electrode is previously calibrated in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ saturated electrolyte with respect to an in situ reverse hydrogen electrode (RHE), by using platinum wires as working electrodes, which yields the relation $\mathrm{E(RHE)}=\mathrm{E(SCE)}+0.279\mathrm{~V~}$ at $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution.1f,2c A glassy carbon electrode with a diameter of 3 mm covered by a thin catalyst film was used as the working electrode. Typically, $4~\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst was suspended in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ water-isopropanol solution with volume ratio of 3:1 with 50 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion solution to form a homogeneous ink assisted by ultrasound for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then ${5}~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the ink was spread onto the surface of glassy carbon electrode (loading $0.285~\mathrm{mg~cm^{-2}}$ ) by a micropipette and dried under room temperature. Then, $3~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ Nafion solution was drop-cast on top to protect the film. Electrochemical measurements were conducted in $0.5{\bf M}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ phosphate buffer and $1\ \mathrm{M\KOH}$ , respectively. For Linear Sweep Voltammetry (LSV) measurements, the scan rate was set to be ${5\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{s}}^{-1}$ . The polarization curves were plotted as overpotential $(\eta)$ vs log current $(\log J)$ to get Tafel plots for assessing the HER kinetics. The Tafel slope $(b)$ was obtained by fitting the linear portion of Tafel plots to the Tafel equation $(\eta=b\log\left(J\right)+a)$ . Primary test shows that data with and without iR compensation are identical (Figure S1), so all data were reported without iR compensation.1g,2c,g
2.5. Photocatalytic HER. The photocatalytic hydrogen production experiments were performed in a closed $50~\mathrm{mL}$ flask vertically irradiated by a $300\mathrm{~W~}$ high-pressure xenon lamp (PLS-SXE300UV, Beijing Trusttech. Co. Ltd.). UV light ( $220–400\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $36.5\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{-2}$ at $365~\mathrm{nm}$ ) were separated by UV-ref optical filter. Visible light ${\tt>}400\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $43.0\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $420\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{nm}})$ were separated by UV-IR-cut filter. For EY-sensitized reactions under visible light, $1.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of sulfides or $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(20\mathrm{wt\%})$ and $3.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of EY dye were stirred in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of TEOA- $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solution $(5\%,\mathrm{v/v)}$ . For semiconductor catalytic reactions, $0.2~\mathrm{mg}$ of sulfides or in-situ photodeposited Pt,1c,d $20~\mathrm{\mg}$ of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ , $20~\mathrm{\mL}$ of $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H(T E O A)–H_{2}O}$ solution $(5\%,\mathrm{v/v})$ and UV(visible) light irradiation were used. Prior to the irradiation, the reactor was deaerated by bubbling Ar for $30~\mathrm{min}$ to remove air. The amount of evolved $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was monitored by a gas chromatograph (Bruker, GC-450, TCD, Ar carrier). $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was from Degussa: Hulls Corporation. $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ was synthesized by calcining urea at $600~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ according to literature.6d
2.6. Density Functional Theory Calculations. The electronic structures and adsorption free energy were computed by Vienna ab initio Simulation package (VASP) using spin-polarized density functional with the Hubbard model $(\mathrm{DFT+U})$ ).10a,b The projector augmented wave (PAW) model with Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) function was employed to describe the interactions between core and electrons, and the value of the correlation energy (U) was fixed at 5.9, 7.5, 6.4 and $4.0\mathrm{eV}$ for the 3d orbits of Co, Zn, Ni and Cu, respectively.10c,d An energy cutoff of $550~\mathrm{eV}$ was used for the plane-wave expansion of the electronic wave function. The Brillouin zones of all systems were sampled with Gamma-point centered Monkhorst-Pack grids. A $3\times3\times3$ and $3{\times}3{\times}1$ Monkhorst Pack $\mathbf{k}$ -point setup were used for bulk and slab geometry optimization, while $9{\times}9{\times}9$ for the density of states and electronic band structure. The force and energy convergence criterion were set to $0.02\ \mathrm{eV}\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ and $10^{-5}~\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. Molecular model-building details and free-energy calculations are given in the SI.
## 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
3.1. Formation of Homogenous Bimetallic MOFs. Bimetallic MOFs (designated as MCo-MOFs, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M=Zn}$ , Ni and $\mathrm{Cu})$ ) were synthesized via room-temperature co-precipitation reaction of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $\displaystyle\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ mixtures with 2-methylimidazole in methanol (see Experimental for details in SI). Theoretically, $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ can be homogenously introduced in Co-MOFs framework by substituting ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites due to their similar ionic radius and electronegativity (Figure 1a, Table S1 and Table S2).9d,e XRD patterns of all MCo-MOFs samples exhibit strong diffraction peaks at identical positions similar to Co-MOF (Figure 1b and Figure S2). The atomic ratios of $\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{C}_{}\mathbf{o}$ detected by ICP in as-synthesized samples are very close to those in the starting materials (Table S3), indicating the effective co-precipitation of all metal ions with 2-methylimidazole. Additionally, the color of both ZnCo-MOFs (even at molar ratio of ${Z_{\mathrm{n}}}^{2+}/{{\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}}{=}1_{,}$ ) and Co-MOF are purple, very different from the white color of Zn-MOFs (Figure S3). This phenomenon visually confirms that $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions are incorporated in the frame of Co-MOF instead of separate $Z\mathrm{n-MOF}$ phase. SEM images (Figure 1c-f) show that the as-synthesized MOFs are highly uniform polyhedra with size of ca. $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Different view positions of a single particle (Figure 1d, inset) reveal that it has a well-defined rhombic dodecahedral morphology.
3.2. Formation of Homogenous Bimetallic Cobalt Sulfides. The formation of homo-bimetallic MOF provides a chance to fabricate homo-bimetallic sulfides. In detail, the bimetallic sulfides can be produced by in-situ topotactic transformation of MOFs in TAA solution, as follows:
$$
\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CSNH}_{2}+\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{3}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{OC}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})\cdot\mathrm{SH}
$$
$$
\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{^{2}H_{3}(N H_{2})C(O C_{2}H_{5})\mathrm{-}S H+C_{2}H_{5}O H\rightarrow C H_{3}(N H_{2})C(O C_{2}H_{5}}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{4H_{2}S\ +M_{\it x}C o_{3-x}(C_{4}H_{5}N_{2})_{8}\rightarrow M_{\it x}C o_{3-x}S_{4}+8C_{4}H_{6}N_{2}}
$$
Upon further thermal annealing in $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere, the rhombic dodecahedral morphology of MOF precursors is well maintained but the crystallinity is greatly improved (Figure 2a, Figure S4 and Figure S5).
The formation of hollow structure can be discerned unambiguously from some cracked particles (Figure 2b). TEM image provides further insight into the hollow interior and detailed structure (Figure 2c). The well-defined inner cavities inside uniform polyhedra are clearly elucidated by the sharp contrast between the center and the edge. The shell has a thickness of ca. $30{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ and is robust enough to endure the sulfidation reaction and subsequent thermal annealing. High-resolution TEM images (Figure 2d and Figure S5b) reveal that the shell is composed of nanoparticles of ca. $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The hollow polyhedrals afford high BET specific surface area (125, 109, 121 and $147~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) and large pore volume (0.91, 0.81, 0.87 and $1.17~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{{S}_{4}}}}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}.$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , respectively).
XRD pattern (Figure 3a) shows that the sulfides can be indexed as cubic $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ (JCPDS No. 47-1738). Particularly, the incorporation of second metals (Zn, Cu and Ni) do not affect the spinel structure, and there is no new peak originating from other metal sulfides, indicating the homogenous incorporation of metal ions into the lattice of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ by taking the sites of Co ions (Figure 3b, c). Moreover, the peaks of doped $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ do not show any shift compared to those of pristine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , suggesting the doping almost does not influence the cell parameters due to the very similar ionic radius of guest and host metal ions. The uniform distribution of metal and S species is also confirmed by EELS element mapping (Figure 2e), and the $\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{C}_{}\mathbf{o}$ ratio in sulfides is very close to that of bimetallic MCo-MOF precursors (Table S3). XPS analysis also excludes the existence of carbon and nitrogen residues in the sulfides (Figure S6).
