Time-dependent experiments were carried out to monitor the formation process of hollow sulfides (Figure 4a). At the beginning of sulfidation procedure, ${\mathrm{S}}^{2-}$ ions released from TAA upon hydrolyzation quickly react with metal ions on MOFs surface to form a thin layer of sulfides, which acts as a physical barrier to prevent the direct contact and chemical reaction between outside sulfide ions and inner metal ions. EELS element mapping (Figure 4b) confirms the formation of $\mathrm{MOF}@\$ metal sufide core/shell structure. Then, metal and/or sulfide ions have to diffuse through this newly formed sulfide layer to continue the sulfidation reaction. Since the ionic radius of metal ions $(72{\sim}74~\mathrm{pm})$ is significantly smaller than that of ${\mathrm{S}}^{2-}$ ions $(184~\mathrm{pm})$ (Table S1), the outward diffusion of metal ions is much easier than the inward diffusion of ${\mathrm{S}}^{2-}$ ions. Therefore the inner organic framwork is gradually dissolved and the released metal ions penetrate the pre-formed shell to react with ${\mathrm{S}}^{2-}$ ions on the outer surface, which finally produces a hollow void inside the shell. In this case, the MOF precusors serve as a self-template to produce hollow polyhedra morphology. Importantly, this strategy can be extended to fabricate other hollow binary- and mono-metal sulfides with tunable compostion using corresponding MOF as the precursor (Figure 3a, Figure 4c, d, Figure S4, Figure S7b and Table S3).
3.3. Superior Electrocatalytic Performance in Acidic Media. The homogenous incorporation of second metal in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , different from the surface doping/heterojunction,2c,5a,11a provides a good platform to investigate the relationship between activity and composition. The electrocatalytic HER activity of $\mathrm{M}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ in acidic media $\mathrm{(pH=0)}$ was evaluated with commercially available $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst $(20~\mathrm{\wt\%})$ as reference. The bimetallic sulfides show obviously enhanced performance compared with $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , with the overpotential at $10\mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\ ,$ $(\eta_{10})$ decreasing from $380~\mathrm{mV}$ for pristine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ to $243~\mathrm{mV}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , $186~\mathrm{mV}$ for $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , and $80~\mathrm{mV}$ for $\mathrm{{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{{S}_{4}}}}$ , (Figure 5a). For $\mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ with different $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}/C_{0}$ molar ratio, an optimal HER catalytic activity presents when $x=0.3$ , with the lowest onset overpotential of $35~\mathrm{mV}$ (Figure S8) and a rapid cathodic current increases at higher bias along with vigorous evolution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bubbles from the electrode surface (Video. SI). $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ electrode also produces a catalytic current density of $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ with a low overpotential of $129~\mathrm{mV}$ . By plotting overpotential $(\eta)$ against log current $\left(\log\ J\right)$ , the kinetic parameters of HER were calculated. The linear part of Tafel plots (Figure 5b) reveals Tafel slopes of $\sim30.4$ , ${\sim}85.3$ and ${\sim}47.5~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{\calZn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , respectively. This suggests that hydrogen evolution over $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ proceeds via the Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism and the electrochemical desorption step is rate-limiting. By further applying extrapolation method to the Tafel plots (Figure S9), the exchange current density $(J_{0})$ of $0.15\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ was obtained for $\mathrm{{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{{S}_{4}}}}$ , which is 6 times larger than that of pristine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ $(0.025\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})$ . It is worth noting that such high catalytic activity, ie, low overpotential, low Tafel slope, and high exchange current density of hollow $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ polyhedra is much better than most of the reported noble-metal-free HER catalysts (with similar catalyst loading) including transition metal sulfides, selenides, phosphides, carbides and nitrides, and metal-free carbon-based materials (Table S4).
Figure 5c compares the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generated over hollow $\mathrm{\calZn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ versus theoretically possible $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ amount that can be evolved from electrochemical HER. $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ shows not only a stable HER performance but also a rate (49.7 $\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})$ very close to the theoretically achievable value. In other words, $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ gives about $100\%$ Faradaic yield and has excellent stability in electrochemical HER. Actually it retains the activity and morphology even after 60 hours testing (Figures 5d and Figure S10), with no change in the polarization curve after 1000 cycles (Figures 5d, inset). In contrast, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ shows evident decreased HER activity after 10 h, with the retention of current density about $75\%$ (Figure S11). Therefore, $Z\mathrm{n}$ doping not only enhances HER activity but also significantly promotes the stability of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ .
When $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ hollow polyhedra are converted into powders by ball milling (Figure S12), the activity is decreased evidently with $\eta_{10}$ of $125~\mathrm{mV}$ (Figure 5a), indicative of the advantage of hollow structure. Even though, the $\mathrm{\calZn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ powders still show much better activity than hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ . Furthermore, electrochemical double layer capacitance (Figure S13a) reveals a higher electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) of bimetallic sulfides (46.9, 43.1, 41.5 and $41.3~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ for $\mathrm{\calZn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , milled $\mathrm{\calZn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , respectively) than $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ $(32.5~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ ). Note that pure $Z\mathrm{nS}$ exhibits negligible HER activity (Figure S7c). Therefore, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ is the major catalyst and the homogenous doping of $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions contributes to the catalytic performance. It is also noted that the catalyst without thermal annealing has a low activity due to the poor crystallinity (Figure S1).
3.5. Universal Electrocatalyst over a Wide pH Range. Until now most non-noble-metal HER electrocatalysts work well only in acidic medium to catalyze the conversion of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ , and the HER activities in alkaline and neutral media to catalyze the conversion of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ are usually about two to three orders of magnitude lower than in acidic medium.6a,13 However, many water-alkali and chloro-alkali HER electrolyzers need active and durable catalysts that can work in alkaline medium, and many microbial electrolysis cells typically need electrocatalysts that can function well in neutral medium. As shown in Figure 5a and Figure 7a, b, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ exhibits very similar activity when the $\mathsf{p H}$ value changes from 0 to 14, which suggests that $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ -based material may be a good candidate to work at all-pH-value. Actually, it is reported that the HER activity of amorphous $\mathrm{CoS}_{x}$ is almost independent of pH value.6a
3.6. Excellent Photocatalytic HER Performance. Hollow $\mathrm{\calZn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ also works very well as cocatalyst in photocatalytic HER. In photocatalysis, dyes and semiconductors can absorb light, generate and transfer electrons to cocatalyst for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution (Figure S15). Figure 7e shows a promising $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of $155.2~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ over $\mathrm{{Zn}_{0.30}\mathrm{{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{{S}_{4}}}}$ when sensitized by Eosin $\mathrm{\DeltaY}$ dye under visible light, which is comparable to the activity of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $\mathrm{\langle188.7~\upmumol~h^{-1}\rangle}$ . Furthermore, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate is very stable during long-term test (Figure S16). $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ are promising UV- and visible-response semiconductors,1h,i,7d respectively (Figure S17). When combined with them, $\mathrm{\calZn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ shows HER activity very close to $\mathrm{Pt}$ (Figure 7f).
## 4. CONCLUSIONS
A general MOF self-templated method to synthesize hollow bimetallic sulfide $\mathrm{(M}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{S}_{4})$ polyhedra has been established. The second metal ions are homogenously dispersed in the lattice of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ due to the homogenous composition of MOF precursor. The incorporation of second metal and the formation of hollow structure improve the catalytic active sites, optimize the electrical conductivity and hydrogen adsorption of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ . The bimetallic sulfides exhibit significantly improved electrochemical and photocatalytic HER activity compared with pristine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ . Particularly, $\mathrm{\calZn}_{0.30}\mathrm{Co}_{2.70}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ shows electrochemical HER activity over a wide $\mathsf{p H}$ range $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{pH}=0-14)$ better than most of the reported non-noble metal catalysts and photocatalytic HER activity comparable to Pt. The strategy developed here, i.e. homogenous doping and self-templated hollow structure, provides a way to synthesize transition metal sulfides for catalysis and energy conversion.
